## Thoughts are not facts

Everyone has odd or unpleasant thoughts at times. In OCD, the brain treats them as dangerous. Learning to notice a thought, label it as **"that is OCD"** and let it pass can reduce its power.

## Delay and reduce compulsions

Trying to stop compulsions all at once can feel impossible. Instead, try **delaying** a compulsion by a few minutes, or doing it **a little less**. Work on this with your therapist if you can.

## Cut down on reassurance

Asking others, or searching online, for reassurance brings short relief but feeds OCD. It can help to agree a plan with family to gently reduce reassurance.

## Look after yourself

Stress, tiredness and low mood can make OCD worse. Regular sleep, activity and time for things you enjoy all help.

## Family and friends

OCD affects the people around you too. **OCD-UK** and **OCD Action** have information for families and carers, and run support groups.
